According to a new report from Intel Market Research, the global Micro PCD Drills market was valued at USD 68.86 million in 2025 and is projected to reach USD 104 million by 2034, delivering a robust CAGR of 6.1% during the forecast period (2025‑2034). This expansion is propelled by the accelerating adoption of advanced semiconductor packaging, a surge in electronic miniaturisation, and the need for lightweight yet high‑strength components in automotive and aerospace sectors.
Micro PCD (Polycrystalline Diamond) drills are ultra‑precision cutting tools manufactured by sintering synthetic diamond particles under high pressure and temperature. Their exceptional hardness, thermal stability and wear resistance enable the machining of ultrahard substrates such as ceramics, composite laminates, and advanced semiconductor wafers. Typical applications include precision hole drilling for semiconductor cleaning fixtures, micro‑through holes in electronic modules, and fine‑hole creation in aerospace‑grade composites. Compared with conventional carbide micro‑drills, PCD variants deliver significantly longer tool life, superior cutting stability and improved hole‑wall integrity, which translates into lower total cost of ownership for end users.
